her Africa\\\" amount to $1.972 million.\\n4 Since December 1992, data for all other republics of the former U.S.S.R. included in \\\"Other Europe.\\\"\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [827, 1664, 1490, 1775], \"page_id\": 72}], \"content\": \"5 Before January 2001, data included in United Kingdom.\\n6 Includes Serbia and Montenegro, which presently form an entity not formally recognized by the United States. Data for entities of the former Yugoslavia recognized as independent states by the United States are reported under \\\"Other Europe\\\" as follows: since December 1992 for Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia and Slovenia; and since June 1994 for the former Yugoslavia Republic of Macedonia.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [667, 108, 967, 137], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1455, 106, 1495, 137], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"73\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [330, 205, 1304, 257], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"CHART CM-A.\\u2014U.S. Liabilities to Foreigners\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [446, 259, 1186, 332], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 408, 1066, 1794], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1089, 405, 1464, 646], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"In the first quarter of 2001, U.S. banking liabilities fell $30 billion, due to decreases opposite Europe and the Caribbean financial centers. Last year, U.S. banking liabilities increased $103 billion, due entirely to strong increases opposite the Caribbean financial centers.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1089, 669, 1475, 997], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking liabilities to foreigners, excluding long-term securities, are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that nearly one-half of U.S. banking liabilities currently is reported opposite the United Kingdom and the banking centers in the Caribbean. Foreigners domiciled in the rest of Europe and in Asia hold an additional 40 percent.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1089, 1018, 1472, 1252], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking liabilities in the mid-1990s went through a growth spurt. The annual growth rate between 1993 and 1997 averaged 10 percent. In 1998 and 1999, growth slowed to less than 5 percent per year, more in line with the 1989 through 1992 period. In 2000, growth increased to 7 percent.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 1810, 1031, 1874], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"1 Includes Bahamas, Bermuda, British West Indies, Netherlands Antilles and Panama. 2 Beginning January 2001, Cayman Islands replaced British West Indies in reporting format.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 113, 171, 137], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"74\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [668, 113, 962, 137], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [197, 184, 1435, 219], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"SECTION II.\\u2014Claims on Foreigners Reported by Banks in the United States\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [532, 255, 1098, 288], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"TABLE CM-II-1.